<% @page_description = "Smartfire privacy policy: what each self-hosted workspace stores, who can see it, and how Google sign-in, Calendar, and Drive data is handled." %>

<h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
<p class="public-updated">Last updated: <%= PublicPolicy.effective_date %></p>
<p class="public-lede">
  Smartfire is self-hosted software. This policy describes how the workspace you
  use handles your data. The organization hosting your workspace controls that
  data; the people who build Smartfire do not receive your workspace's content
  simply because you use the software.
</p>

<div class="public-callout">
  <p>
    <strong>Contractors and guests, please note:</strong> everything you post in
    this workspace, including direct messages, is stored on the workspace's
    server, may remain in its backups, and can be accessible to the people who
    operate that server. It does not disappear automatically when you leave.
    Room permissions limit what ordinary members can see, but they are not
    end-to-end encryption and are not a privacy guarantee against the hosting
    organization.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>Data this workspace stores</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Account and profile:</strong> your name, email address, avatar, role,
    and room memberships. Password sign-in stores a password verifier, never your
    plain password.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Workspace content:</strong> messages in rooms, threads, and direct
    messages, reactions, and files you upload, which are stored on the
    workspace's server. Content you post in a room is visible to that room's
    members; direct messages are visible to their participants.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Sign-in and device data:</strong> a session cookie that keeps you
    signed in, interface preferences kept in your browser, and, where you enable
    them, installed-app (PWA) data and push-notification subscriptions.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Connection and security records:</strong> IP addresses, browser and
    device information, and operational logs used to run the service, investigate
    abuse, and keep accounts secure.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Who can see workspace content</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Room members</strong> can see content posted in rooms they belong to.</li>
  <li>
    <strong>Workspace administrators</strong> manage accounts and workspace
    settings. That role alone does not let them open private rooms or direct
    messages they do not belong to through Smartfire's interface.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>People with access to the server, database, or backups</strong> can
    access stored workspace data, including direct messages and uploaded files.
    This infrastructure access is separate from room membership and the
    application's administrator role.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Configured bots, agents, and webhooks</strong> can receive messages and
    attachment links in rooms they belong to, limited by their capability grants.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  The public pages describing Smartfire (About, Privacy, Terms) set no tracking
  cookies and load no analytics.
</p>

<h2>Optional integrations</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>GitHub:</strong> where enabled, pasting a pull-request link shows a
    live card fetched from GitHub; members who link their own GitHub account can
    act on pull requests as themselves. Room subscriptions can post repository
    events into rooms.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Huddles:</strong> voice and video calls connect through the real-time
    media server configured for the workspace. Call preferences, such as device
    choices, are kept in your browser.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Error diagnostics:</strong> the workspace operator may configure error
    reporting (Sentry) for its own deployment. When configured, crash reports go
    to the operator's error-reporting account. Reports can include selected
    record fields, including message text when a message cannot be displayed,
    as well as error and request details. Default personal data collection is
    left off, but that does not exclude these diagnostic fields.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  Hosting, backup, and media providers depend on the organization running your
  workspace; this policy makes no promise about specific vendors or regions.
</p>

<h2>Signing in with Google</h2>
<p>
  Where the workspace enables Google sign-in, Google verifies your identity and
  Smartfire receives your stable Google account identifier, your verified email
  address and Workspace domain, and your name for setting up your profile. The
  request uses only the <code>openid email profile</code> scopes. No Google access
  tokens or refresh tokens from sign-in are kept. Google sign-in never connects
  your calendar or your Drive files.
</p>

<h2>Google Calendar (optional)</h2>
<p>
  Connecting Google Calendar from your profile is opt-in. Smartfire keeps your
  encrypted access and refresh tokens, your connection email address, and the
  granted scopes on the workspace server, and writes private copies of Smartfire
  events you are Going to or Maybe attending onto your primary Google Calendar,
  including the title, description, time, and meeting links. Publishing is one
  way: Smartfire never reads your existing calendar contents, and changes you
  make in Google do not sync back.
</p>
<p>
  Disconnecting removes the connection and its local entries from the workspace
  server and attempts to remove the calendar copies it created, though some
  remote copies may remain for you to delete. Your Google sign-in link is
  separate and stays in place. You can also revoke access any time from your
  <%= link_to "Google Account connections page", "https://myaccount.google.com/connections", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>;
  revoking stops future access but does not delete events already created, file
  grants already made, messages already posted, or your sign-in identity.
</p>

<h2>Google Drive (optional)</h2>
<p>
  Where enabled, pasting a Drive link can show a preview chip (file name, type,
  owner display name, modified time, and link) resolved at view time with the
  <em>viewer's own</em> Google credentials; people who cannot open the file see
  only the plain link. Preview metadata is cached briefly (about five minutes)
  per viewer and never shared across members. Attaching a Drive file to a
  message stores only the file's ID on the workspace server, never its name or
  contents.
</p>
<p>
  The file-sharing picker works entirely in your browser with per-file consent:
  it uses a short-lived token kept only in browser memory, never the tokens
  stored on the server for Calendar. It reads the selected file's details,
  whether you may share it, and the existing permission list, then grants reader
  access only to the current room members you explicitly choose, keeps any
  stronger access they already have, and sends no Google sharing notification
  emails. Grants apply immediately and stay in place even if you never send the
  message or a recipient later leaves; later members are not added
  automatically.
</p>
<p>
  These Drive features never download or store your file contents; files you
  upload directly to Smartfire are a separate feature stored on the workspace
  server. Google's own policies apply to Google's services.
</p>

<h2>How Google user data is protected</h2>
<p>
  Data obtained through Google's sign-in, Calendar, and Drive scopes is used
  only to provide the visible features described above, to keep the service
  secure, and as the law requires. It is not sold, not used for advertising, not
  used to judge credit or lending applications, and not used to train
  general-purpose AI models. People may read Google user data only with your
  affirmative agreement to view specific data, when necessary for security or
  legal compliance, or for internal operations using aggregated, anonymized
  data as Google's Limited Use requirements permit. Infrastructure access does
  not authorize additional uses of Google user data. Drive attachments are
  shared with configured room bots and agents only as file IDs and links under
  their existing room access, never as Google credentials or file contents.
  Smartfire follows Google's
  <%= link_to "API Services User Data Policy, including its Limited Use requirements", "https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>.
</p>

<h2>Retention and deletion</h2>
<p>
  The workspace keeps messages, uploads, and account data until they are
  removed. The hosting organization decides retention and backup practices for
  its workspace, so copies may persist in backups after visible content is
  deleted, and there is no promise of instant or complete deletion everywhere.
  Leaving the workspace or having your account deactivated does not
  automatically delete your messages, uploads, or backup copies.
  To ask about access, correction, or deletion of your data, contact the
  organization hosting your workspace, which handles such requests subject to
  its legal and business obligations.
</p>

<h2>Contact</h2>
<% operator_name = PublicPolicy.operator_name %>
<% contact_email = PublicPolicy.contact_email %>
<% if operator_name %>
  <p>This workspace is operated by <strong><%= operator_name %></strong>.</p>
<% else %>
  <p>
    This workspace is operated by the organization hosting it. Your workspace
    administrator can tell you who that is.
  </p>
<% end %>
<% if contact_email %>
  <p>Privacy questions for this workspace: <%= mail_to contact_email %></p>
<% else %>
  <p>Direct privacy questions to your workspace administrator.</p>
<% end %>
